Q: C# WebView2 and viewing local HTML files with YouTube embedded in &lt;iframe&gt; A: <p>Your second solution seems to work Bionic it's an excellent solution thanks a lot. I am not sure how it does though so I will investigate further. I was getting worried and I thought that I would have to use HttpListener and create a simple HTTP server. I made a change or two and here is the function I am using for testing</p>
<pre><code>        private async void testfunc()
        {
            // Call this line once in your application 
            // to properly initialise the control
            await webView21.EnsureCoreWebView2Async();

            // Example based on Microsoft Docs
            string htmlRootDirectory = &quot;D:\\Users\\Terry\\Desktop\\MyApps\\localhtmlviewer&quot;;
            webView21.CoreWebView2.SetVirtualHostNameToFolderMapping(
                &quot;appassets.example&quot;, htmlRootDirectory, CoreWebView2HostResourceAccessKind.DenyCors);
            webView21.Source = new Uri(&quot;https://appassets.example/testvideo.html&quot;);
        }
</code></pre>

Q: Missing MEDIA_LIBRARY permissions appears suddenly after months of usage A: <p>OS has final say on which permissions would be granted or denied at particular time and expect that app should lost particular permission at any OS updates.</p>
<p>Don't block permission, instead let app gracefully work around permission state.</p>
<pre><code>const [permissionResponse, requestPermission] = MediaLibrary.usePermissions({
  writeOnly: true,
  granularPermissions: ['photo'],
});



if(permissionResponse.status !== &quot;granted&quot; || !permissionResponse.canAskAgain){
  // show error to indicate that user can use Media library
  return;
}

